    {
      "kind": "greeting",
      "start": 0.71,
      "end": 2.07,
      "text": "Hi! How are you today?"
    },
    {
      "kind": "reply",
      "start": 9.96,
      "end": 15.47,
      "text": "Those runners are baby plants on a leash. You can either snip them off or use them to make new plants for free."
    },
    {
      "kind": "reply",
      "start": 18.68,
      "end": 25.31,
      "text": "Pin the little plantlet onto a pot of compost with a bent wire or a stone. Once it's rooted in a few weeks, cut the runner."
    },
    {
      "kind": "reply",
      "start": 27.87,
      "end": 34.31,
      "text": "If you leave too many on, the mother plant puts energy into them instead of fruit. Keep two or three per plant, cut the rest."
    },
    {
      "kind": "reply",
      "start": 41.02,
      "end": 46.35,
      "text": "About three or four years before they tire out. That's why rooting runners every summer keeps the bed going."
    }
  ]
